====Adjective====
{{wikidata lexeme|L3348}}
{{en-adj|further,more,er|sup=furthest,most,~most,est}}

# Designating the side of the body which is positioned to the east if one is facing north, the side on which the [[heart]] is not located in most humans. This [[arrow]] points to the reader's right: →
#: {{syn|en|right-hand|dexter|dextral}}
#: {{ant|en|left|left-hand|sinister|sinistral}}
#: {{par|en|starboard}}
#: {{ux|en|After the accident, her '''right''' leg was slightly shorter than her left.}}
# [[clockwise|Clockwise]], particularly when describing a change in direction or orientation.
#: {{ux|en|The road up ahead contains a '''right''' bend.}}
#: {{ux|en|Rotate the bolt to the '''right''' to tighten it.}}
# Complying with justice, correctness, or reason; [[correct]], [[just]], [[true]]. ''See also the [[right#Interjection|interjection senses]] below.''
#: {{syn|en|correct|just}}
#: {{ant|en|wrong|incorrect|unjust}}
#: {{ux|en|That's not the '''right''' thing to do.}}
#: {{ux|en|So I was '''right''' all along? [[c'mon|C'mon]]. I want to hear you say it.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1610|author=w:John Locke|title=An Essay Concerning Human Understanding|section=Book II
|passage=If there be no prospect beyond the grave, the inference is certainly '''right''', "Let us eat and drink, for to-morrow we die."}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1808|author=Bishop {{w|Joseph Hall}}|title=Devotional works
|passage=there are some dispositions blame-worthy in men, which are yet, in a '''right''' sense, holily ascribed unto God; as unchangeableness, and irrepentance.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1841|author=Charles Dickens|title=Barnaby Rudge|chapter=13
|passage=What do you send me into London for, giving me only the right to call for my dinner at the Black Lion, which you’re to pay for next time you go, as if I was not to be trusted with a few shillings? Why do you use me like this? It’s not '''right''' of you. You can’t expect me to be quiet under it.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=March 6 2007|author=w:Julie Rutterford|title=[[:w:Life_on_Mars_(British_TV_series)|Life on Mars]]|section=[[:w:List_of_Life_on_Mars_(British_TV_series)_episodes|Season 2, Episode 3]]
|passage=''Sam Tyler:'' Look, look, you know when I said I wasn't wrong? Well, I was. But I was '''right''' about this not being the IRA. I was '''right''' to follow my instincts. Like you said, go with your gut feeling. I'm just taking your lead.<br>''Gene Hunt:'' So I'm '''right'''.<br>''Sam Tyler:'' We both are.<br>''Gene Hunt:'' Right.<br>''Sam Tyler:'' Right.<br>''Gene Hunt:'' Just [[as long as]] I'm more '''right''' than you.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=January 4 2018|author=Catherine Ford|title=Religious-Based Health Care Raises Ethical Questions|titleurl=http://calgaryherald.com/opinion/columnists/ford-religious-based-health-care-raises-ethical-questions|journal=Calgary Herald
|passage=But when that patient requests access to medical care that violates some religious tenet, is it '''right''' that he or she either be denied outright or forced to seek an alternative facility?|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20251229024816/https://calgaryherald.com/opinion/columnists/ford-religious-based-health-care-raises-ethical-questions|archivedate=29 December 2025}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2024 January 10|author=Christian Wolmar|title=A time for change? ... just as it was back in issue 262|journal=RAIL|issue=1000|page=61|text=Of course, I was not always '''right'''. I questioned the value of Crossrail (a scheme revived by Prescott after being scrapped by the Conservatives), suggesting wrongly that it may be "doomed to hit the buffers" {{...}}. A dozen years later, I published my book on it, extolling the line's wonders. We are all allowed to change our minds.}}
# {{senseid|en|appropriate}} [[appropriate|Appropriate]], perfectly [[suitable]]; [[fit for purpose]].
#: {{ant|en|wrong}}
#: {{ux|en|Is this the '''right''' software for my computer?}}
# [[healthy|Healthy]], [[sane]], [[competent]].
#: {{ux|en|I'm afraid my father is no longer in his '''right''' mind.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1913|author={{w|Edgar Rice Burroughs}}|title={{w|The Return of Tarzan}}|publisher=Ballantine Books|location=New York|year_published=1963|page=202|passage=“If I didn’t know the dear old fellow so well by now,” Tennington remarked to Miss Strong, “I should be quite certain that he was—er—not quite '''right''', don’t you know.”}}
# Real; veritable (used emphatically).
#: {{ux|en|You've made a '''right''' mess of the kitchen!}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2016|author=Sarah Harvey|title=A Laugh-out-loud Modern Love Story
|passage=He's got a wicked sense of fun, he can be a '''right''' laugh, he's ever so broadminded – ooh, and he's got a lovely broad chest too.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1670|author=w:John Milton|title=The History of Britain
|passage={{...}}in this battle and whole business the Britons never more plainly manifested themselves to be '''right''' barbarians: no rule, no foresight, no forecast, experience, or estimation}}
# {{lb|en|geometry}} Of an angle, measuring 90 degrees, or one quarter of a complete rotation; the angle between two [[perpendicular]] lines.
#: {{ux|en|The kitchen counter formed a [[right angle|'''right''' angle]] with the back wall.}}
# {{lb|en|geometry}} Of a geometric figure, incorporating a right angle between edges, faces, axes, etc.
#: {{ux|en|a [[right triangle|'''right''' triangle]] &emsp; a '''right''' prism &emsp; a '''right''' cone}}
# {{lb|en|geography}} Designating the bank of a river (etc.) on one's right when facing downstream (i.e. facing forward while floating with the current); that is, the south bank of a river that flows eastward. If this arrow: ⥴ shows the direction of the current, the tilde is on the right side of the river.
#: {{ant|en|left}}
#: {{ux|en|The Louvre Museum is on the '''right''' bank of the [[Seine]].}}
# Designed to be placed or worn [[outward]].
#: {{ant|en|wrong}}
#: {{ux|en|the '''right''' side of a piece of cloth &emsp; Begin this stitch on the '''right''' side.}}
# {{lb|en|politics}} Pertaining to the political right; [[conservative]].
#: {{syn|en|right-wing|conservative}}
#: {{ant|en|left|left-wing|liberal}}
# {{lb|en|Australia}} [[all right|All right]]; not requiring [[assistance]].
#: {{syn|en|all right|OK}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1986|author=David Williamson|chapter=What If You Died Tomorrow|title=Collected plays|volume=1|publisher=Currency Press|page=310
|passage=''Kirsty:'' I suppose you're hungry. Would you like something to eat?<br>''Ken:'' No. I'm '''right''', thanks.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2001|author=Catherine Menagé|title=Access to English|publisher=National Centre for English Language Teaching and Research, NSW: Sydney|page=25
|passage=When the sales assistant sees the customer, she asks ''Are you '''right''', sir?'' This means ''Are you all right?'' She wants to know if he needs any help.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2001|author=Morris Gleitzman|title=Two weeks with the Queen|publisher=Pan Macmillan Australia|page=75
|passage='You lost?'<br>Colin spun round. Looking at him was a nurse, her eyebrows raised. / 'No, I'm '''right''', thanks,' said Colin.'}}
# {{lb|en|dated}} Most favourable or convenient; fortunate.
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=c. 1707|title=w:Joseph Addison|journal=The Tatler
|passage=The lady has been disappointed on the '''right''' side.}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} [[straight|Straight]], not bent.
#: {{ant|en|bowed|crooked|curved}}
#: {{ux|en|a '''right''' line}}
# Of or relating to the [[right whale]].
#* {{RQ:Poe Pym|passage=In the course of the day we saw several large whales of the '''right''' species, and innumerable flights of the albatross passed over the vessel.}}

