# {{alternative form of|en|good morning#Noun}}.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1883|author=[[w:Alfred M. Mayer|Alfred M{{quote-gloss|arshall}} Mayer]]|chapter=Bob White, the Game Bird of America|editor=Alfred M. Mayer|title=Sport with Gun and Rod in American Woods and Waters|volume=II|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:The Century Company|The Century Co.]]|page=672|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/sportwithgunrodi02maye/page/672/mode/1up|oclc=1158760|passage=But if they are greeted with the sunrise and good weather, they cheep a '''good-morning''' to one another in soft, cheerful voices, and go at once to their feeding-grounds, where they regale themselves on the wheat of the stubbles, the buckwheat, the seeds of grasses, and the rag-weed, and on the berries of the haw, the gum, and the chicken-grape.}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} To [[say]] {{m|en|good morning}} (to someone).
#: {{alti|en|good morning#Verb|goodmorning#Verb}}
#* {{RQ:Tolkien Hobbit|edition=revised|page=19|passage=“Good morning!” he said at last. “We don’t want any adventures here, thank you!{{nb...}}” By this he meant that the conversation was at an end. / “What a lot of things you do use ''Good morning'' for!” said Gandalf. “Now you mean that you want to get rid of me, {{...}} And you do know my name, though you don’t remember that I belong to it. I am Gandalf, and Gandalf means me! To think that I should have lived to be '''good-morninged''' by Beladonna Took’s son, as if I was selling buttons at the door!”}}
#* {{RQ:Adams Long Dark Tea-Time of the Soul|pages=83–84|pageref=83|passage=The orderly arrived promptly in his well-pressed loose green tunic, '''good-morninged''' cheerfully, and bustled around locating bedroom slippers and dressing gown.}}

===Interjection===
{{en-intj}}

# {{archaic form of|en|good morning#Interjection}}.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1808|author=Noah Bisbee|title=The History of the Falcos. A Comedy, in Four Acts.|volume_plain=[https://www.proquest.com/books/history-falcos/docview/2138580873/se-2 part first]|location=Walpole, N.H.|publisher={{...|Printed for the author, at the}} Observatory Press|section=act 3, scene 2|page=80|oclc=41438878|passage=Sturnus Corvus, and Caius Alaudus. '''Good-morning''', gentlemen. / Gallinarius Falco and Caius Graculus. '''Good-morning'''. / Caius Alaudus. '''Good-morning''' monsieur Falco. / Gallinarius Falco. '''Good-morning''', you little fawning, meeching, creeping, sneaking, stinking puppy-dog.}}
#* {{RQ:Twain Innocents Abroad|chapter=III|pages=33–34|pageref=33|passage=“'''Good-morning''', Sir. It is a fine day.” {{...}} “'''Good-morning''', Sir. It is a fine day for pleasuring.{{nb...}}”}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1872|month=September|title=Through William Penn’s ‘Low Counties’|journal=[[w:Lippincott's Monthly Magazine|Lippincott’s Magazine of Popular Literature and Science]]|volume=X|issue=17|location=Philadelphia, Pa.|publisher=[[w:J. B. Lippincott & Co.|J{{quote-gloss|oshua}} B{{quote-gloss|allinger}} Lippincott and Co.]]|page=260|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/sim_mcbrides-magazine_1872-09_10/page/260/mode/1up|column=1|issn=2578-563X|oclc=1779490|passage=He would always say, with grave cheer, ‘'''Good-morning''', madam!’ to which Mary would reply, with her pure Quaker simplicity, ‘Art thou well, George?’ and pass on, to spread her napkined butter and cheeses in the quaint, breezy colonnades which you remember on Market street.}}

